C - RECURSION
==============

Function calling itself.


BASIC RECURSION
----------------
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0)
        return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}


HOW IT WORKS
------------
factorial(5)
  = 5 * factorial(4)
    = 5 * 4 * factorial(3)
      = 5 * 4 * 3 * factorial(2)
        = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * factorial(1)
          = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 * 0!
          = 120


BASE CASE
---------
Every recursion needs a base case.
Where the function stops calling itself.

if (n == 0)     ← Base case
    return 1;


RECURSIVE CASE
---------------
The function calls itself with smaller input.

return n * factorial(n - 1);   ← Recursive call


EXAMPLES
--------
Count down:
void countdown(int n) {
    if (n == 0)
        printf("Done!\n");
    else {
        printf("%d\n", n);
        countdown(n - 1);
    }
}


WHEN TO USE
-----------
Tree structures
Divide and conquer
Factorial, Fibonacci
File system traversal


COMMON ERRORS
-------------
No base case → Stack overflow
Never reaches base → Infinite recursion
Too deep → Stack overflow


TIPS
----
Always have base case.
Always progress toward base case.
Test with small numbers.
Recursion can be slow.
Loops often faster.
